* Check context and namespaces
@code bash
kubectl config current-context
kubectl config get-contexts
kubectl get namespaces
@end
Switch with `kubectl config use-context context_name`. The following recipes use an explicit namespace; replace `my-namespace`, `pod_name`, and `app` with your values.

* Find pods and inspect a failure
@code bash
kubectl get pods -n my-namespace -o wide
kubectl describe pod pod_name -n my-namespace
kubectl get events -n my-namespace --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
@end
Start with pod status, then scheduling/probe errors and recent events.

* Follow logs or inspect the previous container
@code bash
kubectl logs pod_name -n my-namespace -c app --tail=100 -f
kubectl logs pod_name -n my-namespace -c app --previous
@end
`-c app` selects the container; `--previous` reads its last terminated instance when available.

* Forward a service to localhost
@code bash
kubectl port-forward -n my-namespace service/app 8080:80
@end
Open `http://localhost:8080`. Here 80 is the service port; Ctrl-C closes the forwarding session.

* Inspect a deployment rollout
@code bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/app -n my-namespace --timeout=120s
kubectl rollout history deployment/app -n my-namespace
kubectl describe deployment app -n my-namespace
@end
A timeout does not cancel the rollout. Check pod events and logs to find the cause.

* Launch a temporary interactive network-debugging pod
@code bash
kubectl run net-test -n my-namespace --rm -it --restart=Never --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- /bin/bash
@end
Exit the shell to remove the pod. Cluster policy may restrict this image or its capabilities.

Related: {:docker:}[docker]. Reference: {https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/}[kubectl quick reference].
